  {
    "@id": "https://ticketpro.ca/event/1695912657",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Event"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/startDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-09-15T19:30:00-03:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/location": [
      {
        "@id": "https://ticketpro.ca/place/55150121"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://tproatlantic.ticketpro.ca/api/v1/resources/1695925352.jpeg?version=2"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/offers": [
      {
        "@id": "https://tproatlantic.ticketpro.ca/genid/0c8af36dd54daf2a"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://tproatlantic.ticketpro.ca/en/pages/1695925402"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://tproatlantic.ticketpro.ca/en/pages/1695925402"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@value": "Burnside Brewing Presents: Ben Caplan"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@value": "<p>Ben Caplan has always defied easy categorization. With his deep, commanding baritone and\nelectrifying stage presence, he merges the raw energy of diverse folk traditions with modern\ninnovation. His music isn't just performed; it's experienced. Now, with The Flood, Caplan takes\nhis artistry to new depths, crafting an album that meditates on endings, resilience, and the art of\ncarrying wisdom forward.</p>\n\n<p>Scheduled for release in May 2025, The Flood is both timely and timeless. 'The world is ending\nfor someone all the time,' Caplan muses. 'But endings aren't absolute. Cultures, ecosystems,\ncivilizations—they evolve, adapt. This album is about finding the pieces that survive and\ncarrying them forward.'</p>\n\n<p>Musically, The Flood is a collision of past and future. Melodically tilted towards the folk traditions\nof Eastern Europe, the album reinvents these sounds with timeless lyrics and modern\nproduction. Clarinet melodies soar over pulsing synthesizers; grand pianos give way to\nsequenced rhythms. The result is an intricate sonic tapestry—both ancient and urgent.\nWith nearly 2,000 performances across 27 countries, Caplan's live shows are known for their\nraw energy and emotional depth. The Flood is designed with the stage in mind—its seamless\ntransitions and dynamic arrangements promise an immersive concert experience.</p>\n\n<p>'Music holds space for people. My hope is that this project allows listeners to confront the grief\nof endings, but also find joy in what's ahead.'</p>\n\n<iframe width='560' height='315' src='https://www.youtube.com/embed/FRD0W2-tr64?si=84Os4Fxo6zsUbKi_' title='YouTube video player' frameborder='0' allow='accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share' referrerpolicy='strict-origin-when-cross-origin' allowfullscreen></iframe>\n\n\n<style>\n.location-page-temp {display: block!important;}\n@media screen and (max-width: 600px){iframe{width:100%;}}\n</style>\n\n"
      }
    ],
